"""
Swing-up and balancing of the Furuta pendulum with a nonlinear MPC in the balancing role.

The structure is that of `SwingupCatch`: the energy-based `EnergySwingup` pumps the
pendulum up, `NearTop` switches to the balancing controller once the pendulum is within
`catch_angle` of upright, and the velocities are estimated from the angles with the
discrete `VelocityEstimator`. What differs is the balancing controller. Instead of the
`LQRstabilizer` -- a state feedback designed around a linearization of the plant -- an
`MPCComponents.ACADOSMPC` solves, every tick, a finite-horizon optimal control problem
over the *nonlinear* multibody `QubePendulum` model itself, with the motor voltage
bounded to ±`umax` and the arm angle bounded to the end stops.

The prediction model is the very plant model: `furuta_mpc_dynamics()` (src/mpc.jl)
compiles a `QubePendulum` with `MultibodyComponents.multibody` and hands the result to
`continuous_dynamics` with the `ForwardDiff` Jacobian backend. A multibody model compiled
this way references cached linear solves (MTK diffcache parameters) that cannot be rebuilt
symbolically, which is what rules out the default `Symbolic` backend and, with it, C export
-- this controller runs on the Julia solver backend only. The four MPC states are the
model's states, named so the state input does not depend on the compiler's state order:
the two joint angles and their derivatives, which are exactly what the hardware measures
and what the estimators produce.

The cost is quadratic in the deviation from upright -- the reference is the upright state
`[0, π, 0, 0]` -- with the infinite-horizon LQR cost-to-go of the model linearized at
that point as terminal weight (`terminal_lqr_cost`), so a short horizon (`Np` intervals of
`Ts`) behaves like the infinite-horizon regulator around upright while the constraints
are honoured along the horizon. The NLP is solved by one real-time iteration per tick by
default (`SQP_RTI`), which takes well under a millisecond here.

The measured elbow angle is wrapped to `[0, 2π)` before it reaches the MPC, so the
reference `π` is upright whichever way the pendulum came up.

The end stops are the MPC's constraint while it is in command; during the swing-up the
same `ErrorRecovery` as in `RuntimeController` pulls the arm back once it passes the limit.
"""
component FurutaMPC
  "Measured arm (shoulder) angle"
  shoulder_angle = Dyad.RealInput() {^shoulder_angle}
  "Measured pendulum (elbow) angle, 0 hanging down"
  elbow_angle = Dyad.RealInput() {^elbow_angle}
  "Control command"
  u = Dyad.RealOutput() {^u}
  "acados status of the last MPC solve: 0 success, 2 maximum iterations, 4 QP failure, ..."
  exitflag = Dyad.RealOutput() {^exitflag}
  "1 while the MPC is in command (pendulum within catch_angle of upright), 0 during swing-up"
  stabilizing = Dyad.RealOutput() {^stabilizing}
  velocityestimator_shoulder = QuanserComponents.VelocityEstimator() {^velocityestimator_shoulder}
  velocityestimator_elbow = QuanserComponents.VelocityEstimator() {^velocityestimator_elbow}
  anglenormalization = QuanserComponents.AngleNormalization() {^anglenormalization}
  energyswingup = QuanserComponents.EnergySwingup(final umax = swingup_umax, final arm_centering_gain = arm_centering) {^energyswingup}
  neartop = QuanserComponents.NearTop(final th = catch_angle) {^neartop}
  errorrecovery = QuanserComponents.ErrorRecovery(final arm_limit = arm_limit) {^errorrecovery}
  mpc = MPCComponents.ACADOSMPC(dynamics = dynamics, states = FURUTA_MPC_STATES, outputs = FURUTA_MPC_STATES, Ts = Ts, Np = Np, Q1 = Q1, Q2 = Q2, umin = [-umax], umax = [umax], constrained = FURUTA_MPC_ARM_SIGNAL, constrained_min = [-arm_limit], constrained_max = [arm_limit], soft_weight = arm_soft_weight, terminal_lqr_cost = true, operating_point = [0, pi, 0, 0], nlp_solver = nlp_solver, warm_start = warm_start, integrator = MPCComponents.ACADOSIntegrator.ERK(), jacobian_backend = MPCComponents.ACADOSJacobianBackend.ForwardDiff(), backend = MPCComponents.ACADOSBackend.Julia(), max_iter = max_iter, levenberg_marquardt = levenberg_marquardt) {^mpc}
  zero = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(k = 0) {^zero}
  upright = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(k = pi) {^upright}
  switch1 = BlockComponents.Logical.Switch() {^switch1}
  stabilizing_flag = BlockComponents.Math.BooleanToReal() {^stabilizing_flag}
  """
  Prediction model of the MPC: a `ContinuousDynamics` of the Furuta pendulum built with an
  AD Jacobian backend (see `furuta_mpc_dynamics`). The default is the `QubePendulum` with
  the identified parameters
  """
  structural parameter dynamics::Native = furuta_mpc_dynamics()
  "Sample time of the controller; the MPC's shooting interval, so it must equal the clock period"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.005
  "Prediction horizon in shooting intervals; the horizon is Np * Ts"
  structural parameter Np::Integer = 30
  "Motor saturation [V]: the MPC's control bound. The amplifier range is ±10 V"
  structural parameter umax::Real = 10.0
  "Arm angle of the end stops [rad]; the MPC keeps the arm inside ±arm_limit (softly, over its horizon). The same limit the swing-up state machine treats as out of bounds"
  structural parameter arm_limit::Real = 1.9198621771937625
  """
  Quadratic penalty on the slack of the arm constraint. Moderate on purpose: the pendulum can
  come up with the arm already past the limit (in simulation, where nothing stops it), and
  with acados' default of 1e6 the constraint then dominates the cost and the MPC drops the
  pendulum to haul the arm back; at 1e3 it balances first and recovers the arm after, while
  still holding an arm that starts inside
  """
  structural parameter arm_soft_weight::Real = 1e3
  "NLP solver: one real-time iteration per tick (SQP_RTI, the default) or SQP to convergence"
  structural parameter nlp_solver::MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver = MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver.SQP_RTI()
  """
  Initial guess of the NLP at every tick. `None` (the default) restarts the solver's trajectories
  from the current state every tick, so one bad solve -- the MPC is solved on every tick, also
  during the swing-up, on states nowhere near upright -- cannot poison the next: with `Shift`
  a single NaN iterate persists in the warm start and the solver never recovers
  """
  structural parameter warm_start::MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart = MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart.None()
  "Maximum SQP iterations per tick (SQP only). Structural here, since acados sizes its memory by it"
  structural parameter max_iter::Integer = 10
  "Levenberg-Marquardt regularization of the Gauss-Newton Hessian"
  structural parameter levenberg_marquardt::Real = 1e-3
  """
  State weight of the MPC, on the deviation from upright in the order [shoulder_angle,
  elbow_angle, shoulder_velocity, elbow_velocity]. The default is the `design_lqr` weighting
  (with a heavier pendulum-angle term); the ratio to `Q2` matters, since the velocities the MPC
  is fed are filtered first differences with half a sample of lag -- a control penalty a
  thousand times smaller destabilizes the loop
  """
  parameter Q1::Real[4, 4] = diagonal([1000.0, 100.0, 1.0, 1.0])
  "Control weight of the MPC"
  parameter Q2::Real[1, 1] = diagonal([100.0])
  "Pendulum angle from upright below which the MPC takes over from the swing-up [rad]"
  parameter catch_angle::Real = 0.4
  "Saturation of the energy swing-up's command [V]"
  parameter swingup_umax::Real = 2.5
  "Arm centering gain of the energy swing-up [V/rad]; stronger than `SwingupCatch`'s default so the arm stays near the end stops instead of running past them while the pendulum is pumped up"
  parameter arm_centering::Real = 5.0
relations
  connect(elbow_angle, velocityestimator_elbow.pos, anglenormalization.u) {^id1}
  connect(anglenormalization.y, energyswingup.elbow_angle, mpc.x[2], neartop.u) {^id2}
  connect(shoulder_angle, energyswingup.shoulder_angle, velocityestimator_shoulder.pos, mpc.x[1], errorrecovery.shoulder_angle) {^id3}
  connect(velocityestimator_shoulder.vel, energyswingup.shoulder_velocity, mpc.x[3]) {^id4}
  connect(velocityestimator_elbow.vel, energyswingup.elbow_velocity, mpc.x[4]) {^id5}
  # The reference is the upright state: arm centred, pendulum at π, at rest.
  connect(zero.y, mpc.r[1], mpc.r[3], mpc.r[4]) {^id6}
  connect(upright.y, mpc.r[2]) {^id7}
  # MPC in command near the top, energy swing-up otherwise. The swing-up has no notion of the
  # end stops, so its command passes through the same `ErrorRecovery` the swing-up state
  # machine uses: beyond the arm limit a proportional pull toward centre replaces it.
  connect(neartop.y, switch1.u2, stabilizing_flag.u) {^id8}
  connect(mpc.u[1], switch1.u1) {^id9}
  connect(energyswingup.realoutput, errorrecovery.u_swingup) {^id10}
  connect(errorrecovery.u, switch1.u3) {^id14}
  connect(switch1.y, u) {^id11}
  connect(mpc.exitflag, exitflag) {^id12}
  connect(stabilizing_flag.y, stabilizing) {^id13}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/SwingupCatch.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "shoulder_angle": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 150, "y2": 250}}}
    },
    "elbow_angle": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    },
    "u": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}},
    "exitflag": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 150, "y2": 250}}}
    },
    "stabilizing": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 750, "y2": 850}}}
    },
    "velocityestimator_shoulder": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 100, "x2": 200, "y1": 250, "y2": 350}}}
    },
    "velocityestimator_elbow": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 100, "x2": 200, "y1": 550, "y2": 650}}}
    },
    "anglenormalization": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 100, "x2": 200, "y1": 400, "y2": 500}}}
    },
    "energyswingup": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 450, "x2": 550, "y1": 650, "y2": 750}}}
    },
    "errorrecovery": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 600, "x2": 700, "y1": 650, "y2": 750}}}
    },
    "neartop": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 450, "x2": 550, "y1": 400, "y2": 500}}}
    },
    "mpc": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 450, "x2": 550, "y1": 150, "y2": 250}}}},
    "zero": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 250, "x2": 350, "y1": 50, "y2": 150}}}},
    "upright": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 250, "x2": 350, "y1": 150, "y2": 250}}}
    },
    "switch1": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 700, "x2": 800, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    },
    "stabilizing_flag": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 700, "x2": 800, "y1": 750, "y2": 850}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
The MPC swing-up controller closed around the physical QUBE, with the hardware I/O inside
the synchronous program -- `FurutaHardware` with `FurutaMPC` in place of the swing-up state
machine.

`HardwareMeasurement` reads the encoders, `HardwareCommand` writes the motor and the
`DataLogger` appends a row per tick in the `MPC_LOG_COLUMNS` order: what was measured and
applied, the loop diagnostics, and the two signals that matter for judging the MPC on the
rig -- acados' `exitflag` and whether the MPC was in command. Compiling this model yields
a synchronous program that needs nothing from its caller but a clock tick; see
`generate_mpc_controller` and `MPCController` in src/mpc.jl.

Unlike `FurutaHardware` there is no homing and no error recovery: the arm limit is a
constraint of the MPC instead, and the arm starts from wherever it is (`open_hardware!`'s
`arm_deg` says where that is).
"""
component FurutaMPCHardware
  measurement = HardwareMeasurement() {^measurement}
  control_system = FurutaMPC(dynamics = dynamics, Ts = Ts, Np = Np, umax = umax, arm_limit = arm_limit, nlp_solver = nlp_solver, warm_start = warm_start, final catch_angle = catch_angle, final swingup_umax = swingup_umax, final arm_centering = arm_centering) {^control_system}
  command = HardwareCommand(umax = umax) {^command}
  diagnostics = HardwareDiagnostics() {^diagnostics}
  logger = DataLogger(n = MPC_LOG_NCOLS, filename = log_file, header = MPC_LOG_HEADER) {^logger}
  periodicclock = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(dt = Ts) {^periodicclock}
  "Controller sample time"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.005
  "Prediction horizon in shooting intervals"
  structural parameter Np::Integer = 30
  "Prediction model of the MPC (see `furuta_mpc_dynamics`)"
  structural parameter dynamics::Native = furuta_mpc_dynamics()
  "Motor saturation [V]: the MPC's control bound, and `HardwareCommand` clamps to it as well"
  structural parameter umax::Real = 10.0
  "Arm angle of the end stops [rad], the MPC's constraint on the arm"
  structural parameter arm_limit::Real = 1.9198621771937625
  "NLP solver of the MPC"
  structural parameter nlp_solver::MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver = MPCComponents.ACADOSSolver.SQP_RTI()
  "Initial guess of the MPC's NLP at every tick (see `FurutaMPC`)"
  structural parameter warm_start::MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart = MPCComponents.ACADOSWarmStart.None()
  "File the log is written to; the driver opens it with this name"
  structural parameter log_file::String = MPC_LOG_FILE
  "Pendulum angle from upright below which the MPC takes over [rad]. Runtime-settable, being one of the compiled program's `TuningGains`"
  parameter catch_angle::Real = 0.4
  "Saturation of the energy swing-up's command [V]. Runtime-settable, being one of the compiled program's `TuningGains`"
  parameter swingup_umax::Real = 2.5
  "Arm centering gain of the energy swing-up [V/rad]. Runtime-settable, being one of the compiled program's `TuningGains`"
  parameter arm_centering::Real = 5.0
relations
  connect(measurement.shoulder_angle, control_system.shoulder_angle, logger.u[2], periodicclock.y) {^id7}
  connect(measurement.elbow_angle, control_system.elbow_angle, logger.u[3]) {^id8}
  connect(control_system.u, command.u) {^id15}
  # The applied voltage is the diagnostics' dependency token as well as a logged column:
  # `exec` is latched by the write, so the reads have to be scheduled after it.
  connect(command.u_applied, diagnostics.dep, logger.u[4]) {^id9}
  connect(diagnostics.elapsed, logger.u[1]) {^id10}
  connect(diagnostics.dt, logger.u[5]) {^id11}
  connect(diagnostics.exec, logger.u[6]) {^id12}
  connect(control_system.exitflag, logger.u[7]) {^id13}
  connect(control_system.stabilizing, logger.u[8]) {^id14}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/FurutaHardware.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "measurement": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 20, "x2": 120, "y1": 136, "y2": 236}}}
    },
    "control_system": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 160, "x2": 260, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "command": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 300, "x2": 400, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "diagnostics": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 440, "x2": 540, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "logger": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 790, "x2": 890, "y1": 140, "y2": 240}}}
    },
    "periodicclock": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 20, "x2": 120, "y1": 10, "y2": 110}}}}
  }
}
end

"""
The MPC swing-up controller closed around the simulated `QubePendulum` -- `FurutaSwingup`
with `FurutaMPC` in place of the swing-up state machine. The plant and the MPC's prediction
model are the same multibody model with the identified parameters.

Compile with `multibody(model, additional_passes = [SynchToolkit.compile_lustre])` and
simulate with `dt = Ts`, as in the README.
"""
component FurutaMPCSwingup
  control_system = FurutaMPC(Ts = Ts) {^control_system}
  qubependulum = QuanserComponents.QubePendulum(idparams = QuanserComponents.identified) {^qubependulum}
  zeroorderhold = DiscreteComponents.ZeroOrderHold(initial_condition = 0) {^zeroorderhold}
  elbow_sampler = DiscreteComponents.SampleWithADEffects(quantized = false, sigma = 0.00001) {^elbow_sampler}
  shoulder_sampler = DiscreteComponents.SampleWithADEffects(quantized = false, sigma = 0.00001) {^shoulder_sampler}
  periodicclock = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(dt = Ts) {^periodicclock}
  world = MultibodyComponents.World(default_body_color = gray, default_rod_color = gray, default_joint_color = gray, nominal_length = 0.1, render = false) {^world}
  parameter gray::Real[4] = [0.9, 0.9, 0.9, 1]
  "Controller sample time"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.005
relations
  connect(elbow_sampler.y, control_system.elbow_angle) {^id9}
  connect(shoulder_sampler.y, control_system.shoulder_angle, periodicclock.y) {^id10}
  connect(qubependulum.shoulder_angle, shoulder_sampler.u) {^id11}
  connect(zeroorderhold.y, qubependulum.voltage) {^id12}
  connect(qubependulum.elbow_angle, elbow_sampler.u) {^id13}
  connect(control_system.u, zeroorderhold.u) {^id14}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/FurutaSwingup.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "control_system": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 200, "x2": 300, "y1": 300, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "qubependulum": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 600, "x2": 700, "y1": 300, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "zeroorderhold": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 400, "x2": 500, "y1": 300, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "elbow_sampler": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 400, "x2": 500, "y1": 550, "y2": 650, "rot": 180}}}
    },
    "shoulder_sampler": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 400, "x2": 500, "y1": 100, "y2": 200, "rot": 180}}}
    },
    "periodicclock": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 200, "x2": 300, "y1": 100, "y2": 200}}}
    },
    "world": {"Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 600, "x2": 700, "y1": 100, "y2": 200}}}}
  }
}
end
